 ## Subtopics
 
-### [Point Colorization via Projective SDF](10792-point-colorization-via-projective-sdf.md)
-Point Colorization via Projective SDF assigns RGB colors to a set of 3D points by projecting them into training views, filtering based on alpha confidence and projective depth consistency, and sampling the closest valid color from the rendered reference frames. It differs from standard nearest-neighbor colorization by enforcing geometric consistency through a projective SDF metric, ensuring that sampled colors correspond to the visible surface rather than occluded geometry or background.
+### Unbounded TSDF Fusion
+Fuses SDF values from multiple frames in contracted space with adaptive truncation, then uncontracts the space [S1, unofficial]. It differs from standard TSDF fusion by handling unbounded scenes via a contraction mapping that compresses infinity into a unit sphere [S1, unofficial]. Choose this when dealing with large-scale outdoor scenes or when the volume of interest extends beyond a fixed bounding box [S1, unofficial]. It calls `compute_sdf_perframe` for each view in the `viewpoint_stack` [S1, unofficial]. The truncation parameter `sdf_trunc` is set to $5 \times \text{voxel\_size}$ [S1, unofficial]. For points outside the unit sphere ($||x|| > 1$), the truncation is scaled by $1/(2-||x||)$ if `inv_contraction` is provided; otherwise, it remains constant [S1, unofficial]. The fusion uses a weighted average update: $new = (old \times w + val) / (w + 1)$, with weights initialized to 1 and incremented per view [S1, unofficial].
 
-### Unbounded TSDF Fusion
-Unbounded TSDF Fusion extends Per-Frame SDF Computation by fusing SDF values from multiple frames in contracted space with adaptive truncation, then uncontracting the space [S1, unofficial]. If an inverse contraction transform is provided, points outside the unit sphere ($||samples|| > 1$) are identified [S1, unofficial]. The base truncation $sdf\_trunc$ is set to $5 \times voxel\_size$ [S1, unofficial]. For points outside the unit sphere, truncation is scaled by $1/(2-||x||)$ [S1, unofficial]. If no inverse contraction is provided, $sdf\_trunc$ remains a constant $5 \times voxel\_size$ [S1, unofficial]. TSDFs are initialized to -1, RGBs to 0, and weights to 1 [S1, unofficial]. The algorithm iterates through a `viewpoint_stack`, calling `compute_sdf_perframe` for each view [S1, unofficial]. The SDF is flattened, and the mask is updated to include points where $SDF > -sdf\_trunc$ [S1, unofficial]. SDF is normalized by $sdf\_trunc$, clamped to $[-1, 1]$, and the mask is applied [S1, unofficial]. TSDF and RGB are updated using a weighted average: $new = (old \times w + val) / (w + 1)$ [S1, unofficial]. Weights are updated: $w_{new} = w_{old} + 1$ [S1, unofficial]. If inverse contraction is provided, samples are uncontracted before returning [S1, unofficial].
+### Signed Ray Distance Function (SRDF)
+A volumetric shape representation where the signed distance of a 3D point $X$ is defined by the depth prediction of a specific camera $j$ along the viewing ray passing through $X$, rather than the shortest distance in any direction [S2]. Unlike standard SDF which uses the shortest distance along any direction, or SDDF which uses a fixed direction, SRDF uses the direction of the viewing ray from a specific camera [S2]. It is defined as $SRDF_j(X) = D_j(X) - Z_j(X)$, where $D_j(X)$ is the predicted depth and $Z_j(X)$ is the point's depth along the ray [S2]. It is differentiable at any point in the 3D observation volume [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: $O(N)$ per frame, where $N$ is the number of points, dominated by matrix multiplication and bilinear sampling [G].
-- **Memory Complexity**: $O(N)$ for storing intermediate tensors (clip coordinates, NDC, masks, sampled values) [G].
-- **Strengths**: Efficiently computes geometric consistency for a single view and integrates seamlessly with multi-view fusion pipelines [G].
-- **Limitations**: Only considers one view at a time and does not handle occlusions from other views within the single-frame computation (this is handled in the fusion step) [G].
-- **When to use**: When initializing or updating SDF fields for a specific training view [G].
-- **When not to use**: When multi-view consistency is required immediately without fusion [G].
+Time complexity is linear with respect to the number of points $N$ for a single view, dominated by the projection and bilinear sampling operations [S1, unofficial]. Memory complexity is $O(N)$ for storing the SDF and RGB values [S1, unofficial]. Performance scales with the resolution of the depth/RGB maps due to the sampling cost, but is generally efficient on GPU due to parallelizable tensor operations [S1, unofficial]. Strengths include simplicity and direct integration with differentiable rendering pipelines [S1, unofficial]. Limitations include sensitivity to depth map accuracy and occlusion handling, as it relies on a single view's depth map [S1, unofficial]. Not suitable for scenes with significant self-occlusion without multi-view fusion [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Per-Frame SDF Computation is part of the 2D Gaussian Splatting pipeline, specifically used in the context of post-processing or refining 3D points using depth information from training views [S1, unofficial]. It originates from the work on 2D Gaussian Splatting by Inria's GRAPHDECO research group [S1, unofficial].
+Originated in the context of 2D Gaussian Splatting and multi-view reconstruction, specifically in the `2d-gaussian-splatting` repository by Inria's GRAPHDECO research group [S1, unofficial]. The method is used to evaluate the consistency of 3D points against 2D observations, facilitating colorization and SDF-based regularization [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the concept of projecting 3D points to 2D to leverage 2D image data for 3D attribute estimation [S1, unofficial].
 
